# 03 — Self-Check Commands & Review Severity

<!-- GEN: Why: turns rules into deterministic PASS/FAIL CLI checks and aligns authors and reviewers on the single BLOCKER/MAJOR/MINOR severity scale. Kind: Mandatory. -->

## 1. Deterministic Pre-Handoff CLI Gates (`PASS` / `FAIL`)

Run on changed files before reporting `READY_FOR_REVIEW`. Every check is binary (`exit 0` = `PASS`, `exit != 0` = `FAIL`):

```bash
# 1. Formatter
{{format_cmd}}

# 2. Linter / Static Analyzer & Complexity
{{lint_cmd}}

# 3. Static Type Checker
{{typecheck_cmd}}

# 4. Forbidden patterns & structure scan via ripgrep (rg)
{{rg_checks}}

# 5. Affected unit tests
{{test_cmd}}
```

## 2. Unified Review Severity Scale (`BLOCKER` / `MAJOR` / `MINOR`)

| Severity | Criteria | Review Verdict Impact |
|---|---|---|
| `BLOCKER` | Failed CLI tool gate (`FAIL`), correctness/security defect, or violation of a mandatory design/contract rule (C1, C2, C3, C4, C11, C12, C14, C15, C16, C17) | `CHANGES_REQUESTED` (blocks approval) |
| `MAJOR` | Maintainability or structural issue not caught by CLI linters (C5, C6, C7, C10, C13) | Must be fixed in the same revision when inside task scope |
| `MINOR` | Redundant "what" comment (C8) or optional readability polish | Optional (never blocks approval) |

Reviewers evaluate only code modified in the current phase diff; legacy debt outside the diff is recorded in `Concerns`, never as a blocker.

